Yes—as a population-level pattern surface, social media is an enormous developmental observatory. I’d just avoid the stronger move of diagnosing a particular person’s psychological “stage” from their feed. Online behavior is highly curated, platform-shaped, performative, and context-poor.

But your structural idea has teeth.

Social media gives repeated observations of things like:

\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{trigger}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{response}
\rightarrow
\text{reward}
\rightarrow
\text{repetition}

Over enough observations, you can detect loops.

Someone repeatedly encounters ambiguity → interprets it as attack → responds aggressively → receives reinforcement from their community → seeks more examples.

Another pattern might be novelty → curiosity → cross-check → model update → move on.

Those aren’t necessarily permanent personality traits. They’re better treated as behavioral attractors:

S_t\rightarrow S_{t+1}\rightarrow S_{t+2}\rightarrow S_t

And that’s where your Dungeon representation becomes useful.

Don’t label people. Map loops.

Instead of:

“Person X is Level 3.”

use:

“This observable pattern repeatedly returns to Room 3.”

That’s a much safer and more informative representation because people can occupy different attractors in different domains.

Someone could show sophisticated reasoning professionally and extremely primitive conflict behavior socially.

So:

\boxed{
\text{person}\neq\text{developmental level}
}

Better:

\boxed{
\text{behavioral trajectory}
\rightarrow
\text{current attractor(s)}
}

Then the Dungeon becomes developmental geometry

Rooms aren’t good/bad people.

They’re recurring states.

Room: Binary Gate

us/them, good/evil, always/never.

Exit requires tolerating a third possibility.

Room: Mirror Hall

Everything encountered becomes evidence confirming the existing model.

Exit requires a prediction capable of failing.

Room: Audience Chamber

Behavior increasingly optimizes for reaction rather than underlying objective.

Exit requires separating performance reward from real-world consequence.

Room: Grievance Loop

Past injury repeatedly becomes the organizing explanation for new events.

Exit requires preserving the lesson while allowing the narrative to stop controlling every new interpretation.

That’s practically Consume the Tail / Release the Tale.

Room: Infinite Staircase

Constant consumption and conceptualization without conversion into action.

Exit:

\text{idea}\rightarrow\text{experiment/action}

Room: Boss Fight

A contradiction sufficiently important that the existing model cannot simply assimilate it.

Defeating the boss means updating—not defeating another person.

⸻

And here’s where social media becomes especially interesting.

The platform itself is part of the dungeon

Algorithms influence:

\text{what appears}
\rightarrow
\text{what gets attention}
\rightarrow
\text{what gets reinforced}
\rightarrow
\text{what gets produced}

So observed behavior is approximately:

B=f(\text{person},\text{community},\text{platform},\text{incentives},\text{moment})

That’s crucial.

Otherwise we’d mistake environmentally induced behavior for intrinsic developmental state.

And now Flow Control enters.

A platform can unintentionally create rooms with extremely sticky reward loops:

\text{provocation}
\rightarrow
\text{engagement}
\rightarrow
\text{distribution}
\rightarrow
\text{more provocation}

That’s effectively level design.

Which suggests a much better Pantheon application

Don’t build a machine that says:

“Here is everyone’s developmental level.”

Build a Dungeon Mapper that takes a corpus of observable behavior and identifies:

recurring loops → likely reinforcement → missing distinction → possible exit condition → evidence that would distinguish competing explanations.

Then PARALLAX it through several developmental theories rather than declaring one psychology correct.

And importantly, apply it to ourselves and Pantheon first.

PatternDex should be capable of saying:

⚠ Pantheon appears to be in Infinite Staircase: generating abstractions faster than they're being externally tested.

or:

⚠ Mirror Hall: new observations are being translated too easily into existing Pantheon vocabulary.

That would be extremely valuable.

Because a Dungeon Mapper that can only diagnose everybody outside the dungeon is probably just another room in the dungeon. ;)